A||N||14||Coop88||Alignment (long)||||9||
Z||000000||Coop88||02-09-2001||02:22 PM||||Took my formula to PepBoys for an alignment after I said hi to a curb. They said I needed a new passenger lower balljoint. They 'aligned' my car. Car didn't pull any more, but I feel a wobble in my floor board at about 40, front end shakes on the interstate at 65. Took my car to Tirebarn for a free balance. They said my rim was bent(Passenger). So I replaced the rim. Didn't help. Lived with it for about a month or two.<P>Started hearing a creaking noise when I backed out of my parking space in the morning. So I checked the wheel, and I could pull on it from 12o'clock. I figured it was the balljoints. After seeing how hard it was to locate the upper balljoints from part suppliers, I thought it would be best to replace the rest of them while I could still buy them. So I did.While I was down there I found that my upper control arm was loose on the driver's side. I tightened it down, and went to the dealership for an alignment since Pepboys gave me this sad story about the bolts being frozen and they couldn't get a wrench on it(this was a different location, the 1st Pepboys closed).<P>So the dealership calls me and says that my car is just too far out, that the car needs to go up on a frame rack. I asked them to explain. They said my frame is bolted in the wrong place, and to fix it, they'd have to drain the cooling system and remove something else that I can't remember. They said it would be easier to just bend the frame. Then they said that they'd talk to their body shop and to call back. I called back later and they said that they lined up the front-"it isn't perfect, but it should drive straight and the tires shouldn't wear". I go pick the car up and take it out on the interstate. The car now pulls in the opposite direction and shakes worse than ever. And yes, I also feel that wobble in my floorboard.<P> I am running out of patience. I have no idea what to do now. Can a bad alignment cause this shaking and wobbling I'm feeling? Tirebarn swears there's nothing wrong with the tires. What could it be? I know it's probably gonna need another alignment, but I don't trust the dealership anymore, and I really don't trust the people at Pepboys. What would you do? Any help would be greatly appreciated. Thank you for your time reading this post. Sorry it was so long.||||reg||9
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||02-09-2001||03:23 PM||||If it's shimming I'd say check for bent rims and tire balance. Yes the tires may be fine, but if their out of balance it can make the car almost undriveable. (driving a friends beater once, going above 45 resulted in such a violent shake that you had to loosly grip the stearing wheel and feel like you were on ice) Also, the loose a-arm could be bad bushings, this will also cause odd handling, possibly some odd noises and strange handeling while braking. other then that, make sure the have the correct alignment settings, there was a miss print in the original print from GM.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||02-09-2001||04:10 PM||||Coop,<P>Are you at home in Indy?<BR>I'll check it out for you if you are!<BR>LMK <BR>Send mail||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||02-09-2001||04:24 PM||||Your frame is bolted in the wrong place and they want to bend the frame in order to align your car correctly?  I could believe you'd hear bullcrap like that from Crackpipe Charlie down at the local Midas, but from a Pontiac dealer, that's inexcusable.<P>There is at least one really good alignment shop here in Columbus (E.T. Paul)...kind of a long drive, but at least you wouldn't get ripped off.<P>I haven't heard such a crock of $hit since the Firestone store on Sunbury Road told me they needed to drill holes in my frame in order to align my '84.  Needless to say I got the hell out of there fast.<P>I hope Satan has a special corner of Hell warmed up and ready for crooked and/or stupid mechanics -- right next door to Hitler.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||02-09-2001||04:33 PM||||first thing is your tire pressure correct.<P>How hard did you hit the curb.  and did you experience this problem after you did?<P>If you didn't have this problem before, then the frame wouldn't have anything wrong with it, unless you hit that curb really hard.<P>I would look at my car from the front on my hands and knees and just to see if the wheels look straight.  <P>2nd, jack the car up and hold the tire at 3-8 and 6 - 12   one is to check ball joints and the other is tie rods.  There should be no play at all.  <P>Check to see if any bushings are worn out causing anything to be loose.  <P>I would just look over everything if it looks right, and compare it to the other side.<P>I took my car to firestone to have my alignment done.  Its 120 bucks and its a lifetime alignment.  They printed a sheet for me of what the original settings were and the ones they adjusted on my car.  I replaced my tierod and i was off so i saw what the difference is.<P>Tires will cause the steering wheel to shake driving faster then 40-50mph when they are out of balance.  <P>Also is your steering wheel pointed foward while your driving in a straight line?||||reg||1
Z||000005||PJ||02-09-2001||05:13 PM||||Did you get a copy of the new alignment settings? If you did what are they? Don't use anyone who won't give you a copy. If anything is loose or sloppy (ball joints, bearings, rod ends) the car will be impossible to align. Shaking and vibrations are usually wheel or tire problems. Swap them around left to right and front to back to try to isolate the problem. I've whacked a number of things with the front of my '88. It takes a very hard hit to bend the subframe (I know because I bent it and replaced it). The weakest part is the upper a-arm. They like to deform near the bushings. Another popular point that gets bent is where the lower ball joint seats on the lower a-arm. This is hard to notice. Also the arm on the knuckle that connects to outer tie rod can bend but the outer tie rod will bend first.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Coop88||02-09-2001||09:43 PM||||My bushings are crap all the way around, but they've been like that since I've had the car. Before the curb, I could take my car up to 100(I don't do this all the time, maybe 3 times)and now 75 is pushing it. I'm not saying it isn't the bushings, I just think it's unlikely.<BR>I seem to be out of room here. I can't scroll down. I'll just post again.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Coop88||02-09-2001||10:00 PM||||I will check my tire pressure again. Just for the record, should I go by what's printed inside my door, or go by the tire pressure on the side of the tire? As far as the wheels, I replaced them. I'll get them rebalanced tomorrow. Also, I have 4 new balljoints up front. I had to replace the passenger lower control arm to put in a new balljoint. The old one was knocked out from the curb. I don't know what my speed was, I was driving through a campus parking lot-to make  a long story short, I didn't make the 90* turn. I had no problems before the curb. Where do I get new alignment settings? The helm manual? As far as the steering wheel, the dealership didn't straighten it out. It looks turned while I'm going straight. Crap. The computer lab's closing. I'll post more after work tomorrow. Thanks for the responses.||||reg||1
Z||000008||skitime||02-09-2001||10:08 PM||||One easy test to eliminate the tires is to put the front on the back or borrow two from another Fiero to test out of round tires.  I had tires that were out of round and I didn't realize it until I had the tire dealer rotate the tires and it was like I was driving on square blocks of wood. The dealer spent many visits to their shop trying to prove to me it was not their tires and not until they put on new tires (different brand) everything was fine.  They ate the two old front tires. I never knew there was a problem when the tires were on the back.  Good Luck||||reg||1
Z||000009||PJ||02-10-2001||12:42 AM||||Coop, forget the air pressure. Redo the bushings with the poly ones. The stock rubber ones are too much work to replace. Besides its fun to burn the old ones out.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||02-10-2001||01:49 AM||||ya chad, there the only place i get mine aligned or balanced. There the only place I know of in Columbus that will balance them on the car. Makes a huge difference over 100 mph. Ive found out most dealers and shops anymore dont have any idea how to align a Fiero or just too lazy to change shims on a-arm shaft. Usually vibration is caused by balance or tire/rim. Misalignment will usually just make it pull one way, hard to control, or do wierd tire wear. ET Paul also trues tires. I have no shake clear up to top speed.<P>try putting on a pair of wheels/tires from another Fiero that you know drives fine, see what yours does with them.||||reg||1
Z||000011||BOILERMAKER||02-11-2001||04:40 PM||||Coop,<P>Watch out for Matt's Alignment shop downtown Indianapolis.  They helped me out with my alignment with a bent cradle.  i only take my cars to local shops where I grew up (country folks) and they do a very nice job.  If you need references, just email me and we can discuss it further.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Coop88||02-11-2001||10:23 PM||||ok, I can see how bad bushings and the wheels and tires can cause the vibration and noises I'm hearing. I'll check this out when I can get down to Indy, since I'm in school in Muncie now. I talked to the dealership and asked them why they wanna bend my frame. They said that the cradle has shifted and needs to be pulled back in place. They still aren't touching my car again. Just thought I'd show my alignment specs.<P>Before       After            Specified Range<P>Left front<BR>-1.2         -0.3    camber        -0.5  0.5<BR> 6.7          5.4    caster         2.5  3.5<BR>-0.11         0.12    toe          0.05 0.25<P>Right front<BR>-0.3         -0.6    camber         same<BR> 2.4          6.2    caster         same<BR>-0.09         0.13     toe          same<BR>||||reg||1
